{
  "name": "PassedOnUnread",
  "comment": "A format type keeps its raw bytes until first use (ruling 6): a json file read and\nwritten back on, unchanged, is byte-for-byte the same. config.json is spelled with\nunusual whitespace and keys out of order so any re-parse-and-write would reformat.\nEach file's bytes are hashed via its path (sha256 streams the file's content, not any\nparsed value): the Data slot carries the explicit path, so the hash reads the file.\nRed today — today's write reformats config.json's 65 bytes to a 40-byte compact json\n({\"zebra\":1,\"alpha\":\"two\",\"beta\":[3,4,5]}); the two files' sha256 differ.",
